  "articleBody": "Installing new baseboards or window casings seems straightforward, but minor errors early on can lead to obvious gaps and costly rework. Here are common mistakes to watch for when planning your interior trim updates:\n\n- Buying insufficient stock: Ordering exact room lengths without accounting for cuts leaves you short. Always factor in an extra 15 to 20 percent for miter waste and awkward spans.\n- Skipping wood acclimation: Installing lumber fresh from the supply yard causes seams to pull apart later. Wood needs at least 48 hours indoors to adjust to your home's moisture level.\n- Fastening only into drywall: Brad nails shot purely into drywall eventually pull free. Secure trim directly into underlying wall studs or sole plates.\n- Ignoring finished floor heights: Setting baseboards before new carpeting, tile, or hardwood is installed often traps flooring or creates awkward gaps.\n- Rushing corner cuts: Forcing simple 45-degree miters on out-of-square inside corners creates open seams. Coping inside joints yields a far tighter, longer-lasting fit.\n- Accepting warped material: Installing bowed or twisted boards creates uneven lines along the wall. Always inspect stock for straightness prior to fastening.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\nTo protect your investment, confirm all materials are checked for stability, acclimated indoors, and matched to your finished flooring plan before any cutting starts.\n\nIf you want smooth, gap-free trim work completed by an experienced team, reach out to HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
